WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.362
The effective type rules help define which lvalue types may be used to access an object.

2
00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:11.313
These rules matter because C compilers use type and aliasing assumptions when optimizing code.

3
00:00:11.313 --> 00:00:19.246
If code accesses storage through an incompatible type, the program may no longer mean what the developer intended in portable C.

4
00:00:19.246 --> 00:00:21.523
Aliasing mistakes are often subtle.

5
00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:28.502
Two pointers can appear to refer to the same bytes, but the language may not allow access through the chosen types.

6
00:00:28.502 --> 00:00:40.623
Character types have special access rules for inspecting object representation, yet that does not mean arbitrary type punning is safe or that every byte sequence is a valid object of another type.

7
00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:44.222
Alignment requirements are another part of valid access.

8
00:00:44.222 --> 00:00:48.336
Some object types must be accessed at suitably aligned addresses.

9
00:00:48.336 --> 00:00:58.547
Casting a pointer changes the pointer type in the source code, but it does not guarantee that the pointed-to storage is correctly aligned or valid for the target type.

10
00:00:58.547 --> 00:01:01.999
Safer designs avoid casual pointer reinterpretation.

11
00:01:01.999 --> 00:01:06.994
Use explicit serialization when data crosses a file, wire, or storage boundary.

12
00:01:06.994 --> 00:01:12.798
Use byte movement where appropriate and then create real objects through well-defined APIs.

13
00:01:12.798 --> 00:01:20.511
Keep object ownership and representation decisions separate so reviewers can see what is raw data and what is a typed object.

14
00:01:20.511 --> 00:01:35.129
A good review asks which object is being accessed, what its effective type is, whether the access type is permitted, whether the address is suitably aligned, and whether any compiler aliasing assumption could be invalidated by the code.

15
00:01:35.129 --> 00:01:39.096
That discipline keeps low-level code portable and reviewable.
